WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.080
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.050
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.000
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.820
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.460
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.469 --> 00:00:19.500
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.279
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.719 --> 00:00:24.890
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it’s

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.580
the start of another three-part

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.439
mini-series, this time on the theme

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.929
of “stories from North Korea”.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.120
In part one, this episode, we’ll

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.220
be talking about Otto Warmbier,

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:41.059
the 21-year-old American student

00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:43.920
who went on a trip to North Korea

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:46.410
and returned unrecognisable.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.810
In part two, we’ll talk about the

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.519
daylight murder of Kim Jong-un’s

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.059
brother in Kuala Lumpur airport.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:57.440
And in the final part, part three, we’ll

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.740
talk about Kim Jong-il’s obsession with

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.970
cinema, and how this led him to kidnap one

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.780
of South Korea’s most famous directors.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.259
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.770
minute and get right into it.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.640
I can imagine you can all think

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.840
of something that you regret

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.039
doing when you were young.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.079
From that tattoo you now try to cover up

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.740
to betting your friend you could climb up

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:28.340
a large tree, only to fall down and break

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.479
your elbow, making mistakes and learning

00:01:31.489 --> 00:01:34.080
from them is all a part of growing up.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.770
And a holiday, far away from home,

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.550
is the kind of place where mistakes

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.160
are perhaps more likely to happen.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:45.520
Normally, of course,

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:47.620
mistakes aren’t permanent.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.169
A sore head in the morning, perhaps

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.520
a slap on the wrist from a police

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.220
officer for doing something more

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:57.489
serious, but nothing that will forever

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.350
change the trajectory of your life.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.320
That is, unless the mistake

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:05.709
takes place in North Korea.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:09.030
So, let me paint you a picture.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.586
It is the 2nd of January, 2016.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.336
A young man is standing in Pyongyang

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.850
International Airport, waiting to

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.400
board a flight back to Beijing.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.889
His name is Otto Warmbier.

00:02:26.269 --> 00:02:29.090
He is twenty-one years old, a star student

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.050
at the University of Virginia, a dutiful

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.360
son, and the eldest of three children.

00:02:36.259 --> 00:02:38.329
He has just spent five days

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.980
in North Korea as part of a

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.600
tour group, a $1,200 five-day,

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.440
four-night “New Year's Party Tour”.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.410
Tourists are allowed in North

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:53.850
Korea, or at least, back then they

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.570
were, but only as part of a group,

00:02:56.839 --> 00:02:59.189
and under very tight supervision.

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.550
He has seen what all tourists

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.850
see: the vast bronze statues of

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.160
the Kim family, the immaculate

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.990
streets, the carefully choreographed

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.580
displays of loyalty and order.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.080
He has bowed where he was told

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.390
to bow, photographed what he was

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.799
allowed to photograph, and said

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:23.340
nothing that might cause offence.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:24.810
It’s been fun.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.100
Eye-opening.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.770
A world away from his hometown

00:03:28.969 --> 00:03:31.269
of Cincinnati, Ohio, obviously.

00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:34.480
But now he is ready to leave.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:36.999
He is one of the last to go

00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:39.949
through security, and as he hands

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.370
over his passport, the officers

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.700
spend more time looking at his

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.019
documents than seems necessary.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.620
They flick through the pages,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.119
whispering to each other.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:54.329
Some more officials approach from

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:58.030
behind, tap him on the shoulder, and ask

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.870
him to follow them to a private room.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.100
The man he has been sharing a room

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.360
with for the past few days, an

00:04:06.549 --> 00:04:08.690
Englishman named Danny Gratton,

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:11.659
attempts a joke, telling Otto, “'Well,

00:04:11.869 --> 00:04:13.090
that's the last we'll see of you.”

00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:17.130
Unfortunately, it would prove to be true.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.050
This will be the last time anyone outside

00:04:22.090 --> 00:04:25.039
North Korea will see Otto Warmbier, at

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.850
least, as the Otto Warmbier they knew.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:33.350
Now, to understand what happened next, and

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.270
perhaps why, we need to remind ourselves

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.370
of a little bit of North Korean history.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:44.080
North Korea, or to give it its official

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.380
name, the Democratic People's Republic of

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.989
Korea, is, by almost any measure, the most

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.230
closed and controlled state in the world.

00:04:55.109 --> 00:04:58.150
It has been ruled by the same family since

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:03.399
1948: first Kim Il-sung, then his son Kim

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.351
Jong-il, and now his grandson Kim Jong-un.

00:05:08.153 --> 00:05:10.930
The regime maintains control through an

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:14.229
almost total monopoly on information.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.590
There is no free press, no independent

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:20.159
internet access for ordinary citizens,

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:23.450
and no meaningful political opposition.

00:05:24.429 --> 00:05:26.169
Leaving the country without

00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:28.900
permission is illegal and, for most

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.030
people, essentially impossible.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:34.579
For those who break the rules, or

00:05:34.727 --> 00:05:37.400
who are simply accused of doing so,

00:05:37.679 --> 00:05:40.470
the consequences can be devastating.

00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:44.260
Sentences are extremely harsh, and

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:46.799
typically involve back-breaking

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.919
forced labour in the country’s

00:05:48.929 --> 00:05:51.419
network of political prison camps.

00:05:52.059 --> 00:05:54.470
And crucially for our story,

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.739
North Korea is extraordinarily

00:05:57.969 --> 00:06:00.240
sensitive about its image.

00:06:01.209 --> 00:06:04.740
Any perceived insult to the leadership

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.010
of the Kim family, to the state,

00:06:07.329 --> 00:06:09.919
to the symbols of the regime, these

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:13.594
are treated not as a minor offence,

00:06:13.654 --> 00:06:16.980
but as a serious political crime.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.089
And this brings us back to Otto

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:23.809
Warmbier, and what the North Korean

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.419
authorities said he had done.

00:06:27.669 --> 00:06:30.689
For the first six weeks after his

00:06:30.719 --> 00:06:33.789
arrest, the only statement they released

00:06:33.819 --> 00:06:35.909
about him was that he had committed

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.850
"a hostile act against the state".

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.559
To Otto’s family, and to those who

00:06:43.559 --> 00:06:45.679
had been on the tour with him, this

00:06:45.679 --> 00:06:49.189
seemed…implausible, very unlikely.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.689
He was a young man, an

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:53.210
otherwise well-behaved,

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.320
upstanding member of society.

00:06:55.909 --> 00:06:58.370
He had been very cautious on the tour, and

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.439
he knew all too well the consequences of

00:07:02.439 --> 00:07:04.419
getting on the wrong side of the regime.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:08.069
Sure, it was New Year’s Eve, and

00:07:08.089 --> 00:07:10.700
everyone had had a few drinks, but

00:07:11.014 --> 00:07:13.210
there was no indication that he was

00:07:13.249 --> 00:07:16.280
particularly drunk or disorderly, nobody

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:18.929
remembered him causing any problems.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.099
There must surely have been

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:23.360
a mixup, a misunderstanding,

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:26.029
and he would be released soon.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:29.260
But…he wasn’t.

00:07:30.189 --> 00:07:34.250
On the 29th of February, 2016 — two

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.419
months after his arrest — he was brought

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.459
before the cameras for a press conference.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.360
He appeared thin and clearly distressed.

00:07:46.059 --> 00:07:49.940
In a halting, tearful voice, he delivered

00:07:49.989 --> 00:07:53.370
a prepared statement confessing to his

00:07:53.419 --> 00:07:56.590
alleged crimes and begging the North

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.720
Korean government for forgiveness.

00:07:59.509 --> 00:08:01.720
"I have made the worst mistake of my

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:05.199
life," the young man said, almost weeping.

00:08:05.929 --> 00:08:08.411
You can see the entire thing on YouTube,

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.819
and it is pretty painful to watch.

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:15.340
His family and the US government made

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.169
clear that they considered the confession

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.419
to have been made under duress — in other

00:08:21.419 --> 00:08:24.212
words, that he had been forced to say it.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.547
They simply didn’t accept it.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.679
Even disregarding the content

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.636
of the confession, and with our

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:36.059
“English learner’s” hat on, the

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:39.030
language was as if it had been

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.399
written by a non-native speaker.

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:44.669
He said things like, “I came to

00:08:44.669 --> 00:08:47.890
commit this crime task”, and “but

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.200
since my family suffered from very

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.160
severe financial difficulties”.

00:08:53.219 --> 00:08:56.900
This might be grammatically correct

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.629
English, but these are not the

00:08:59.629 --> 00:09:01.719
words of a native English speaker.

00:09:02.169 --> 00:09:05.460
Anyone listening to the speech could

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.630
clearly understand that these weren’t his

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:12.410
words; someone with good but imperfect

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.040
English had drafted the statement.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:20.070
The North Koreans’ claim was this:

00:09:20.999 --> 00:09:23.660
in the early hours of New Year’s Eve,

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.930
Otto Warmbier had entered a staff-only

00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:30.269
area of his hotel and attempted to

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:33.329
steal a political banner from a wall.

00:09:34.259 --> 00:09:36.819
Now, you might think, “ok, he

00:09:36.819 --> 00:09:38.659
had a few drinks and wanted to

00:09:38.659 --> 00:09:40.840
take a fun memento of his trip.

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:42.870
He’s a young man, that’s

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:44.350
not such a terrible crime”.

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:47.090
Well, three things.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:51.520
Firstly, removing any kind of political

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.689
banner in North Korea is a serious

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:57.060
crime, no matter the intention.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:00.670
You might think this law is extreme,

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.650
but it is the law, and a North Korean

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.920
citizen would certainly have been treated

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.939
the same way, if not more harshly.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:13.130
Secondly, the supposed “evidence” for

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:17.210
Otto taking the sign was flimsy at best.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:20.950
There was some grainy CCTV footage

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:24.420
that showed a tall-ish man touching

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:28.229
the sign at around 1am, but, his

00:10:28.229 --> 00:10:30.339
companions on the trip said that they

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:33.129
didn’t get back until more like 3am,

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.230
and what’s more, this corridor was

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.703
well-lit even in the middle of the night,

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.290
which is very unusual in North Korea.

00:10:42.049 --> 00:10:44.300
In other words, this CCTV

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.909
footage was not exactly reliable.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.419
And lastly, and perhaps most bizarrely,

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.280
the North Koreans claimed that Otto

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.890
Warmbier’s actions were not those

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.650
of a tipsy young American looking

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.550
for something fun to hang in his

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.270
dormroom; this was a hostile act,

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.550
carried out at the instigation of

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:11.710
a Methodist church group and the US

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.240
government, as part of a deliberate

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:17.360
attempt to destabilise the country.

00:11:18.179 --> 00:11:20.400
Or at least, that was the official

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:23.279
policy line, and what Otto confessed

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:25.130
to in his prepared statement.

00:11:26.319 --> 00:11:29.520
Now, that version of events was,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.490
to put it gently, implausible.

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:35.880
Firstly, Otto Warmbier was

00:11:35.929 --> 00:11:38.470
Jewish, so he was unlikely to

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:40.650
be part of a Methodist church.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:44.180
And secondly, he was a twenty-one-year-old

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:47.890
student on a five-day group tour; the

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:51.770
idea that he was a covert agent working

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:54.080
with the CIA to bring down the North

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:56.820
Korean government is not one that many

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:59.090
Western observers found convincing.

00:12:00.089 --> 00:12:02.170
But…it didn’t matter.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.870
North Korea was not interested

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:07.280
in convincing anyone, anyone

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.150
outside its borders, that was.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:12.590
It had Otto Warmbier, and there

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:14.930
was nothing anyone else could do.

00:12:16.166 --> 00:12:19.076
A month later, at a trial that lasted

00:12:19.086 --> 00:12:22.626
barely an hour, he was found guilty and

00:12:22.636 --> 00:12:26.776
sentenced to fifteen years of hard labour.

00:12:27.943 --> 00:12:30.293
And then, from the perspective of

00:12:30.293 --> 00:12:33.363
the outside world, he disappeared.

00:12:34.559 --> 00:12:36.609
For the next seventeen months,

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:39.749
his family received practically no

00:12:39.779 --> 00:12:41.909
information about his condition.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:44.150
The North Korean government

00:12:44.569 --> 00:12:47.210
said he was being treated well

00:12:47.219 --> 00:12:49.000
and receiving medical care.

00:12:50.399 --> 00:12:53.160
Now, there’s another aspect to this story.

00:12:53.699 --> 00:12:56.770
The United States has never had

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:59.390
formal diplomatic relations with

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:01.579
North Korea, and the two countries

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.400
are still technically at war.

00:13:05.099 --> 00:13:07.220
So actually having these

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:09.800
conversations was…complicated.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:12.690
All communication went through

00:13:12.769 --> 00:13:15.109
Sweden, which does have an embassy,

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:17.970
and staff in Pyongyang, the capital.

00:13:18.913 --> 00:13:22.469
Otto’s parents, Fred and Cindy Warmbier,

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.019
spoke publicly, pleading for their

00:13:26.019 --> 00:13:28.269
son's release, and trying everything

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.269
they could think of to bring him home.

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:35.849
And then, in June 2017, a year and a

00:13:35.849 --> 00:13:38.829
half after the incident, the North Korean

00:13:38.829 --> 00:13:41.509
government made a surprise announcement.

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:44.740
Otto Warmbier would be released

00:13:44.930 --> 00:13:46.939
on humanitarian grounds.

00:13:48.199 --> 00:13:51.180
He was, they said, in a coma,

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.719
and had been since shortly

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.729
after his trial in March 2016.

00:13:57.209 --> 00:13:58.959
More than a year earlier.

00:13:59.518 --> 00:14:02.568
The reason they gave for the coma was

00:14:02.568 --> 00:14:05.628
something called botulism, which is a rare

00:14:05.628 --> 00:14:08.238
form of food poisoning, and they said that

00:14:08.238 --> 00:14:11.238
this, combined with a sleeping pill he

00:14:11.238 --> 00:14:14.428
had been given, had sent him into a coma.

00:14:15.288 --> 00:14:17.358
Now, we’ll come to whether this

00:14:17.358 --> 00:14:19.978
was actually true later on, but,

00:14:20.328 --> 00:14:22.828
spoiler alert, most foreign doctors

00:14:23.048 --> 00:14:25.208
don’t buy this official explanation.

00:14:26.328 --> 00:14:29.478
On the 13th of June, Otto Warmbier

00:14:29.712 --> 00:14:32.248
arrived back in the United States.

00:14:32.798 --> 00:14:35.158
His parents rushed to meet him, but

00:14:35.698 --> 00:14:38.048
as they approached the aeroplane, they

00:14:38.048 --> 00:14:42.398
heard this raw, almost animalistic

00:14:42.488 --> 00:14:46.448
groaning, like a primordial cry for help.

00:14:47.528 --> 00:14:49.348
It was coming from Otto.

00:14:50.318 --> 00:14:53.228
He was a shadow of his former self.

00:14:53.588 --> 00:14:55.418
His hair had been shaved

00:14:55.418 --> 00:14:57.258
off, he was hooked up with

00:14:57.388 --> 00:15:00.478
tubes, he was blind and deaf.

00:15:00.978 --> 00:15:03.638
He could not speak or respond to commands.

00:15:03.978 --> 00:15:06.258
He showed no signs of awareness

00:15:06.488 --> 00:15:07.718
of the world around him.

00:15:08.808 --> 00:15:11.168
He was rushed to hospital, where the

00:15:11.168 --> 00:15:13.948
doctors said he was in what’s called

00:15:13.978 --> 00:15:16.948
a state of unresponsive wakefulness,

00:15:17.208 --> 00:15:20.128
which is a condition where a person's

00:15:20.128 --> 00:15:22.818
eyes may open and they may breathe

00:15:23.078 --> 00:15:25.678
without assistance, but where there

00:15:25.678 --> 00:15:28.908
is no meaningful conscious activity.

00:15:29.708 --> 00:15:31.508
In other words, he had lost

00:15:31.568 --> 00:15:34.078
virtually all of his brain function.

00:15:35.258 --> 00:15:37.828
And tragically, the doctors told his

00:15:37.828 --> 00:15:40.068
parents, it would never come back.

00:15:40.558 --> 00:15:42.538
There was no hope.

00:15:43.907 --> 00:15:45.727
His parents made the agonising

00:15:45.737 --> 00:15:48.297
decision to remove his feeding

00:15:48.297 --> 00:15:50.717
tube, thus sealing his fate.

00:15:51.487 --> 00:15:54.697
Six days after his return home, on the

00:15:54.697 --> 00:15:59.537
19th of June, 2017, Otto Warmbier died.

00:16:00.387 --> 00:16:01.627
He was twenty-two years old.

00:16:02.967 --> 00:16:05.287
His parents, who had spent seventeen

00:16:05.297 --> 00:16:07.537
months fighting to bring their son

00:16:07.537 --> 00:16:10.927
home, had him back for less than a week.

00:16:12.252 --> 00:16:14.842
The question that followed, and which

00:16:14.842 --> 00:16:17.202
has still never been fully answered,

00:16:17.692 --> 00:16:21.352
was what happened to Otto Warmbier?

00:16:22.452 --> 00:16:24.842
The North Korean government maintained

00:16:25.082 --> 00:16:27.762
that he had been treated well, that they

00:16:27.792 --> 00:16:31.052
bore no responsibility for his condition,

00:16:31.552 --> 00:16:33.802
and that his death was the result

00:16:33.822 --> 00:16:36.662
of the botulism, the food poisoning,

00:16:36.852 --> 00:16:38.712
that he had suffered before his coma.

00:16:39.952 --> 00:16:43.222
American doctors were deeply sceptical.

00:16:43.992 --> 00:16:47.142
If it was caused by botulism, it was

00:16:47.202 --> 00:16:49.512
unlike anything they had ever seen.

00:16:50.432 --> 00:16:53.552
As for his parents, they were, as

00:16:53.582 --> 00:16:56.252
any parent would be, desperate to

00:16:56.252 --> 00:16:59.768
find some kind of justice, to hold

00:16:59.778 --> 00:17:02.998
someone or something accountable

00:17:03.138 --> 00:17:04.858
for the death of their son.

00:17:06.078 --> 00:17:07.901
By this point, there had been

00:17:07.975 --> 00:17:10.138
a change in the White House.

00:17:10.588 --> 00:17:13.358
Barack Obama was out, and Donald Trump

00:17:13.528 --> 00:17:17.318
had taken his place at the start of 2017.

00:17:18.138 --> 00:17:20.618
The Warmbiers knew that Donald Trump

00:17:20.718 --> 00:17:23.968
was a keen watcher of Fox News, and

00:17:23.968 --> 00:17:26.338
thought that if they could go on the

00:17:26.338 --> 00:17:29.108
show and tell their story, it might

00:17:29.158 --> 00:17:31.958
appeal to the one viewer who could do

00:17:31.958 --> 00:17:34.768
something about it: the then relatively

00:17:34.788 --> 00:17:37.348
recently inaugurated President Trump.

00:17:38.358 --> 00:17:41.088
In these interviews, the Warmbiers

00:17:41.268 --> 00:17:42.948
stated that they believed

00:17:43.178 --> 00:17:45.268
their son had been tortured.

00:17:46.268 --> 00:17:48.298
Otto’s father stated that his

00:17:48.308 --> 00:17:50.818
son’s teeth looked like they had

00:17:50.818 --> 00:17:53.228
been rearranged with pliers, and

00:17:53.548 --> 00:17:55.788
that he had a scar on his foot.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:59.320
The Fox interview worked.

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:01.920
It had its intended purpose.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.830
Donald Trump tweeted out “great

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:07.260
interview”, adding that “Otto was

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:09.400
tortured beyond belief by North

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:12.050
Korea”, despite there being no

00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:14.140
evidence that he actually was.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:18.810
Now, the backdrop of all of this was the

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:22.020
escalating war of words between Donald

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:25.250
Trump and Kim Jong-un, after North

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:27.850
Korea’s repeated nuclear weapons tests.

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.550
Some commentators would later claim

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:34.470
that the Otto Warmbier case was

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:38.300
used by Trump to drum up anti-North

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:40.630
Korean sentiment in preparation

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:43.060
for a potential military strike.

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:46.870
Now, this strike never came, thankfully,

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:49.820
but encouraging the idea that North

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:52.490
Korea had tortured Otto Warmbier to death

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.390
certainly helped reinforce this image

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.300
of the regime as brutal and dangerous.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:01.280
But, as you may remember, in

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:04.980
2018, there was an about turn.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.610
After a series of meetings, Donald

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:11.730
Trump declared that he and Kim had

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.630
“fallen in love”, and the so-called

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:17.080
“North Korea crisis” was over.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:21.740
There was now no wider geopolitical

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.920
incentive to push the kingdom over its

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:29.093
treatment of Otto Warmbier, and in 2019,

00:19:29.706 --> 00:19:33.390
Trump publicly stated that he thought Kim

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:36.100
Jong-un didn’t know what had happened.

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.480
In other words, he, and the

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:41.970
North Koreans, were off the hook.

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:45.130
Nobody would see justice, at

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:46.990
least publicly, for whatever

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.060
happened to Otto Warmbier.

00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:53.980
So…what did happen, or at least what

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.790
are some theories about what happened?

00:19:57.760 --> 00:19:59.630
In terms of whether he even

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.540
removed the propaganda poster,

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:04.690
the evidence is inconclusive.

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:08.590
Maybe he did, after a few too many beers.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:11.860
Or it wasn’t him at all; the North

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:14.140
Korean authorities thought it would be

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:16.360
politically useful to have an American

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:19.520
captive, and he was simply unlucky.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.440
And how did he end up in a coma?

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:25.680
The official North Korean

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:27.040
verdict, according to

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.630
American doctors, is unlikely.

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.500
But so is his parents’ claim

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:34.050
that he was badly beaten.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:37.540
His body didn’t show signs of torture,

00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:39.920
American prisoners in North Korea

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:43.030
are generally not tortured, and it

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:45.630
was far more politically useful for

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:48.120
North Korea to have him alive and

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:50.920
healthy than in a vegetative state.

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:54.000
In other words, why would

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:54.870
he have been tortured?

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.500
Nobody stood to gain from it.

00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:01.530
The American doctors stated that his brain

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:04.850
damage, and subsequent coma, were most

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:08.450
likely caused by oxygen deprivation, when

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:10.790
your brain doesn’t get enough oxygen.

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:14.510
This can be caused by physical

00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:17.440
violence, but it can also be caused

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:20.490
by many other things: cardiac arrest,

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:23.350
suffocation, drowning and so on.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.600
To be fair, the American doctors said

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.650
that Otto had otherwise been kept in

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:33.270
good condition, and well looked after.

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:36.990
But whatever had happened, he left

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:40.240
North Korea a shell of the person who

00:21:40.240 --> 00:21:42.280
had arrived seventeen months earlier.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:46.220
There are a few theories about

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:47.440
what might have happened.

00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:51.410
One is that it was some kind of tragic

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:55.050
accident, either a genuine accident or

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:57.980
some miscalculation by one of the guards.

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:01.940
The North Koreans invented the botulism

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:05.030
and sleeping pill excuse, thinking it

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:07.820
might be more plausible than whatever did

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.810
happen, but it had the opposite effect.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:14.170
Another theory was put forward in a

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:17.800
GQ article, and this was that Otto

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:20.580
Warmbier might have tried to kill himself

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:22.623
shortly after hearing his sentence.

00:22:23.097 --> 00:22:25.310
15 years in a labour camp.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:28.540
He was distraught, and might have

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:31.260
tried to end his life before being

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:34.110
found by his guards, but not before

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:36.930
suffering such high levels of oxygen

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:39.800
deprivation that he was sent into a coma.

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:43.180
The truth will almost certainly

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:45.070
never see the light of day.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:48.710
Otto Warmbier was not a spy.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:50.510
He was not a dissident.

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:53.960
He was a young man on holiday who, by

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.560
any reasonable measure, did nothing

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:58.990
that warranted the response it received.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:02.180
And he died for it.

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:05.820
Fortunately for any young Americans who

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.810
might be thinking about making a similar

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:10.340
trip, promising their mothers that things

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:12.410
are different now, and the same thing

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:14.910
would never happen again, they can’t.

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:18.410
The US State Department imposed a

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:21.460
travel ban on US citizens visiting North

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:23.740
Korea shortly after Otto Warmbier’s

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:26.640
death, and North Korea currently

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:29.250
also has a ban on American citizens

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:31.170
entering the country as tourists.

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:35.390
Unfortunately, the story of Otto Warmbier

00:23:35.690 --> 00:23:38.630
does not have a tidy conclusion, in the

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:41.290
sense that justice has not been served

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:43.860
and accountability has not followed.

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.540
Kim Jong-un remains in power.

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:48.050
The regime continues.

00:23:48.410 --> 00:23:49.790
The camps continue.

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:54.220
But what Otto's case did was to make

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:56.990
something vivid and undeniable that

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:59.440
had previously been easy, for many

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:01.910
people, to file away as abstract:

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.530
North Korea is not merely strange or

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:08.760
amusing or ideologically peculiar.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:11.790
A funny hermit kingdom ruled by a

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:14.523
despot with an unconventional haircut.

00:24:14.887 --> 00:24:15.900
It can be genuinely,

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:17.920
systematically, brutal.

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:21.910
And if your name is Otto Warmbier, fatal.

00:24:23.390 --> 00:24:25.260
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:27.596
episode on Otto Warmbier.

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:29.790
As a reminder, this is part one

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:31.660
of a three part mini-series.

00:24:31.910 --> 00:24:34.020
Next up we’ll be talking about the

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:37.090
brazen daylight murder of Kim Jong-un’s

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.590
brother, and in part three it’ll be

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:42.640
Kim Jong-il’s peculiar love for cinema,

00:24:42.930 --> 00:24:45.160
and how that led him to kidnapping

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.420
a famous South Korean director.

00:24:48.181 --> 00:24:50.041
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:50.041 --> 00:24:52.771
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:53.101 --> 00:24:55.961
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:55.961 --> 00:24:58.321
I'll catch you in the next episode.

